    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hadiwijaya"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hadiwijaya.jpg" alt="Hadiwijaya" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hadiwijaya">Hadiwijaya</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince_Raden_Jaka_Tingkir_killing_a_buffalo_under_watch_of_the_Sultan_of_Demak_and_his_retinue_Or._26.639,_Or._7398_2.tiff" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sultanate of Mataram grew from a frontier settlement in the fertile plain of south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, near present-day Yogyakarta. Javanese tradition, recorded in the later <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babad-tanah-jawi">Babad Tanah Jawi</a></em>, relates that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadiwijaya">Hadiwijaya</a> of Pajang granted the forested land of Mataram to his commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ki-ageng-pemanahan">Ki Ageng Pemanahan</a> as a reward for helping to defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arya-penangsang">Arya Penangsang</a> of Jipang in the mid-sixteenth century. Pemanahan settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kotagede">Kotagede</a>, and after his death in about 1584 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panembahan-senapati">Sutawijaya</a>, who had been raised at the Pajang court, refused to pay homage to his overlord. Following the death of Hadiwijaya and the eclipse of Pajang, Sutawijaya ruled from around 1587 under the title Panembahan Senapati ing Alaga, ‘commander in the field’. The chronology of these years rests largely on court chronicles and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> reports written long afterwards, so dates before 1600 are approximate. Senapati subdued Pajang and Demak and campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madiun">Madiun</a> and the East Javanese coastal powers led by Surabaya, though he died in 1601 without controlling the north coast. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panembahan-hanyakrawati">Panembahan Hanyakrawati</a>, remembered as Seda ing Krapyak after his death on a hunt in 1613, continued these wars and prepared the ground for the great conquests of his own successor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-agung">Sultan Agung</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tuban"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tuban.jpg" alt="Tuban" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tuban">Tuban</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masjid_Al-Ikhlas_Desa_Sekaran,_Jatirogo,_Tuban_Setyo_Irman_Faurachmad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Setyo Irman Fa'urachmad, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mataram was an inland, agrarian state whose wealth came from the wet-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields of the volcanic plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>. Most Javanese lived in villages (<em>desa</em>) of peasant cultivators who owed a share of their harvest and labour to the holders of royal land grants, while a court aristocracy of princes and officials, the <em>priyayi</em>, lived on these <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a>. Unlike the commercial port cities of the north coast, which had grown rich on the spice and rice trade, Mataram drew its revenues mainly from land, and its conquest of the coast in the 1610s and 1620s brought devastation and depopulation to former trading centres such as Surabaya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuban">Tuban</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gresik">Gresik</a>. Rice, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and other produce were still exported through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jepara">Jepara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semarang">Semarang</a>, increasingly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a>. Religion combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, adopted by the Javanese courts during the previous centuries, with older Javanese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> traditions: rulers claimed the Islamic titles of sultan or susuhunan and also a mystical bond with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyai-roro-kidul">Ratu Kidul</a>, the spirit queen of the southern ocean. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-agung">Sultan Agung</a>’s calendar of 1633 expressed this synthesis, joining the lunar months of the Islamic year to the Javanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saka-era">Saka era</a>. Court arts, including <em>wayang</em> shadow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, gamelan music and dance, flourished in the royal palace, the <em>kraton</em>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sultan-agung"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sultan-agung.jpg" alt="Sultan Agung" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sultan-agung">Sultan Agung</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anyakrakusuma_of_Mataram.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Basuki Bawono, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mataram’s armies were large levies of peasants raised through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> holders and regional lords, armed with spears, pikes, the kris and bows, and supplemented by firearms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a> acquired through trade. Their strength lay in numbers and in long sieges rather than in technology. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-agung">Sultan Agung</a> (r. 1613–1645) conducted a series of campaigns against the coastal states of the <em>pasisir</em>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lasem">Lasem</a> fell in 1616, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasuruan">Pasuruan</a> in 1617 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuban">Tuban</a> in 1619, and in 1624 his forces conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a>. Surabaya, the strongest rival, surrendered in 1625 after a long siege during which, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> reports, the besiegers cut off its water supply by damming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brantas-river">Brantas</a> river. In 1628 and again in 1629 Agung sent large armies against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a>, the base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-pieterszoon-coen">Jan Pieterszoon Coen</a>, but both sieges failed; in 1629 Dutch ships destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> stores gathered for the army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegal">Tegal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a>, and thousands of soldiers died of hunger and disease. Agung then turned east, conquering the holy town of Giri in 1636 and campaigning against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blambangan">Blambangan</a>, which Balinese forces helped to defend. Later rulers could not command such armies: the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-i">Amangkurat I</a> were defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a>’s rebels at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gegodog">Gegodog</a> in 1676, and from 1677 the dynasty depended on Company troops.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amangkurat-i.jpg" alt="Amangkurat I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-i">Amangkurat I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tegal_Arum_Grave_of_Amangkurat_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">putudananjaya, of Indonesia's Ministry of Education and Culture, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The ruler stood at the centre of a concentric realm. Around the capital (<em>kutagara</em>) lay the core lands (<em>nagara agung</em>), whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields were assigned as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> to princes and officials; beyond them were the outer provinces (<em>mancanagara</em>) of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, and the coastal districts, each governed by regents (<em>bupati</em>) who sent tribute and attended the court at fixed times. A chief minister (<em>patih</em>) supervised the administration. Appanages were not hereditary property and the king could reassign them, but in practice regional lords built their own followings and rebellions were frequent. Royal power relied heavily on the personal authority of the ruler, on marriage alliances and hostages, and on the sacred aura of the court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-agung">Sultan Agung</a> took the title of sultan with the sanction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 1641, while his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-i">Amangkurat I</a> (r. 1646–1677) tried to centralise power by force, killing rival princes and, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> witnesses, having thousands of Islamic teachers and their families massacred around 1648. Later rulers bore the title susuhunan, and their dependence on treaties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> steadily narrowed royal authority, as the Company obtained ports, monopolies, garrisons and a voice in the appointment of senior officials.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kartasura"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kartasura.jpg" alt="Kartasura" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kartasura">Kartasura</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kantor_Kecamatan_Kartasura,_Sukoharjo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The original uploader was Aryphrase at Indonesian Wikipedia., CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mataram created the political and cultural framework of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a> for the following three centuries. Its conquests ended the independence of the commercial port states of the north coast, shifting the centre of Javanese life inland and leaving the coast and its trade increasingly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and to Chinese merchants. The court fostered the distinctive Javanese Islamic culture that the historian M.C. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/m-c-ricklefs">Ricklefs</a> called a ‘mystic synthesis’, combining Islamic identity with reverence for Javanese spiritual forces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-agung">Sultan Agung</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javanese-calendar">Javanese calendar</a> remains in ceremonial use, and the royal cemetery he began at Imogiri, south of Yogyakarta, is still the burial place of the rulers of Surakarta and Yogyakarta. The court chronicles known as the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babad-tanah-jawi">Babad Tanah Jawi</a></em>, compiled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartasura">Kartasura</a> and Surakarta period, shaped how later Javanese understood their past, presenting Mataram as the heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> and Demak. The kraton traditions of etiquette, refined speech levels, wayang and gamelan were inherited by its successor courts. At the same time, the dynasty’s repeated calls for Dutch help set the pattern by which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> became the dominant power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. In modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Agung is honoured for his attacks on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a>, and the Sultan of Yogyakarta, heir of the Mataram line, also serves as governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/special-region-of-yogyakarta">Special Region of Yogyakarta</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trunajaya.jpg" alt="Trunajaya" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_painting_of_Trunajaya_executed_by_Amangkurat_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The collapse of royal authority began under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-i">Amangkurat I</a>, whose violence alienated the nobility, the Islamic teachers and the provinces. In the mid-1670s the Madurese prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a>, allied with Makassarese fighters exiled after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> conquest of Makassar, led a rebellion that took the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plered">Plered</a> in 1677; the old king fled and died on the way to the coast. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-ii">Amangkurat II</a> secured the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> in exchange for ports, revenues and trade monopolies, defeated Trunajaya by 1680 and built a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartasura">Kartasura</a>. The price of survival was rising dependence. Three wars of succession (1704–1708, 1719–1723 and 1746–1757) were each decided with Company troops, and each settlement cost Mataram territory. During the Chinese War of 1740–1743 rebels sacked Kartasura in 1742, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwono-ii">Pakubuwana II</a> regained his throne only by ceding the whole north coast; he then moved the court to Surakarta in 1745–1746. On his deathbed in 1749 he surrendered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the kingdom to the Company. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamengkubuwono-i">Mangkubumi</a> had already risen in revolt, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-giyanti">Treaty of Giyanti</a> of 13 February 1755 divided the realm between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwana-iii">Pakubuwana III</a> at Surakarta and Mangkubumi, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Hamengkubuwana I of Yogyakarta, ending Mataram as a single state.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Mataram, the great inland sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-agung">Sultan Agung</a>, united most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> in the early seventeenth century and shaped Javanese court culture for centuries, but rebellion, succession wars and growing reliance on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> led to its division into Surakarta and Yogyakarta in 1755.</em></p>

